1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,836
January 15th, 1919.

2
00:00:02,836 --> 00:00:03,512
Boston.

3
00:00:03,512 --> 00:00:05,723
It's lunchtime, it's unusually warm for

4
00:00:05,723 --> 00:00:08,533
winter, and people are out enjoying it.

5
00:00:08,683 --> 00:00:10,985
Then they hear a sound like a machine gun.

6
00:00:10,985 --> 00:00:12,135
The ground shakes.

7
00:00:12,135 --> 00:00:14,656
And a wall of something dark and thick, taller

8
00:00:14,656 --> 00:00:17,176
than a two-story house, comes rolling down the

9
00:00:17,176 --> 00:00:19,255
street at 35 miles an hour.

10
00:00:19,405 --> 00:00:20,388
It isn't water.

11
00:00:20,388 --> 00:00:21,324
It's molasses.

12
00:00:21,324 --> 00:00:23,940
And before the day is over, it will kill 21

13
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:24,966
people.

14
00:00:25,016 --> 00:00:27,020
And when it was over, the company would

15
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:27,431
blame...

16
00:00:27,431 --> 00:00:28,792
anarchists.

17
00:00:33,342 --> 00:00:35,556
In 1919 the North End was a crowded,

18
00:00:35,556 --> 00:00:38,386
working-class neighborhood, mostly Italian and

19
00:00:38,386 --> 00:00:41,215
Irish immigrant families packed into tenements

20
00:00:41,215 --> 00:00:42,799
near the harbor.

21
00:00:42,949 --> 00:00:45,425
Right in the middle of it, on Commercial

22
00:00:45,425 --> 00:00:48,272
Street, stood a giant steel tank: 50 feet tall

23
00:00:48,272 --> 00:00:49,448
and 90 feet across.

24
00:00:49,448 --> 00:00:52,058
It belonged to the Purity Distilling Company,

25
00:00:52,058 --> 00:00:54,494
part of a bigger corporation called United

26
00:00:54,494 --> 00:00:56,602
States Industrial Alcohol.

27
00:00:56,752 --> 00:00:58,147
Inside was molasses.

28
00:00:58,147 --> 00:00:59,488
And not for baking.

29
00:00:59,488 --> 00:01:02,019
Molasses could be fermented into industrial

30
00:01:02,019 --> 00:01:04,432
alcohol, which was used to make munitions

31
00:01:04,432 --> 00:01:05,550
during World War I.

32
00:01:05,550 --> 00:01:08,028
That made it very profitable.

33
00:01:08,178 --> 00:01:11,219
The tank went up in 1915, and it was rushed.

34
00:01:11,219 --> 00:01:13,657
The man in charge of building it was a company

35
00:01:13,657 --> 00:01:15,830
treasurer with no engineering background.

36
00:01:15,830 --> 00:01:17,792
He couldn't even read a blueprint.

37
00:01:17,792 --> 00:01:20,169
By most accounts, the tank was never properly

38
00:01:20,169 --> 00:01:22,388
tested by filling it with water before the

39
00:01:22,388 --> 00:01:23,886
molasses went in.

40
00:01:24,036 --> 00:01:26,743
Its steel walls were well under an inch thick.

41
00:01:26,743 --> 00:01:29,132
Engineers who have re-examined the disaster

42
00:01:29,132 --> 00:01:31,631
since say the steel was both too thin and too

43
00:01:31,631 --> 00:01:34,008
brittle for the load it carried.

44
00:01:34,158 --> 00:01:36,607
And from the start, it leaked.

45
00:01:36,857 --> 00:01:39,115
Molasses seeped through the seams so badly

46
00:01:39,115 --> 00:01:41,588
that local families brought buckets to collect

47
00:01:41,588 --> 00:01:42,234
it for free.

48
00:01:42,234 --> 00:01:44,723
Neighbors said the tank groaned and rumbled.

49
00:01:44,723 --> 00:01:46,117
And the company's fix?

50
00:01:46,117 --> 00:01:48,381
They painted the tank brown, so the leaks

51
00:01:48,381 --> 00:01:50,251
would be harder to see.

52
00:01:50,401 --> 00:01:53,251
That cover-up would come back to haunt them.

53
00:01:55,301 --> 00:01:56,749
Now here's the twist.

54
00:01:56,749 --> 00:01:59,484
By January 1919, the war was over, but the

55
00:01:59,484 --> 00:02:02,349
alcohol business was facing a bigger threat:

56
00:02:02,349 --> 00:02:03,130
Prohibition.

57
00:02:03,130 --> 00:02:05,690
The amendment banning alcohol was about to be

58
00:02:05,690 --> 00:02:08,308
ratified, and producers wanted to make as much

59
00:02:08,308 --> 00:02:11,183
as they could while they still had time.

60
00:02:11,333 --> 00:02:12,974
And here's the eerie part.

61
00:02:12,974 --> 00:02:16,326
The amendment was ratified on January 16th,

62
00:02:16,326 --> 00:02:16,716
1919.

63
00:02:16,716 --> 00:02:19,153
The day after the disaster you're about to

64
00:02:19,153 --> 00:02:20,392
hear about.

65
00:02:20,542 --> 00:02:23,512
So the tank was close to full, holding about

66
00:02:23,512 --> 00:02:25,462
2.3 million gallons.

67
00:02:25,612 --> 00:02:27,290
Then the weather got weird.

68
00:02:27,290 --> 00:02:29,615
Just a day or two earlier, Boston had been

69
00:02:29,615 --> 00:02:31,663
bitterly cold, close to zero degrees.

70
00:02:31,663 --> 00:02:34,406
On the morning of the 15th, the temperature

71
00:02:34,406 --> 00:02:35,427
shot up past 40.

72
00:02:35,427 --> 00:02:37,566
A fresh shipment of warmer molasses had

73
00:02:37,566 --> 00:02:39,869
recently been pumped in on top of the cold

74
00:02:39,869 --> 00:02:41,621
stuff already inside.

75
00:02:41,771 --> 00:02:43,158
Warm molasses expands.

76
00:02:43,158 --> 00:02:44,050
It ferments.

77
00:02:44,050 --> 00:02:45,289
It builds pressure.

78
00:02:45,289 --> 00:02:47,724
And all of that was pushing against steel that

79
00:02:47,724 --> 00:02:50,106
was already too thin, held together by rivets

80
00:02:50,106 --> 00:02:52,082
that were already failing.

81
00:02:52,232 --> 00:02:54,887
And at lunchtime on January 15th, the tank

82
00:02:54,887 --> 00:02:57,004
finally ran out of time.

83
00:02:58,954 --> 00:03:01,829
Around 12:30 in the afternoon, the tank gave

84
00:03:01,829 --> 00:03:02,690
way.

85
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:05,384
Witnesses heard a deep rumble, then a rapid

86
00:03:05,384 --> 00:03:07,986
popping as rivets shot out of the steel like

87
00:03:07,986 --> 00:03:08,460
bullets.

88
00:03:08,460 --> 00:03:11,481
The walls split open, and roughly 13,000 tons

89
00:03:11,481 --> 00:03:14,297
of molasses came out all at once.

90
00:03:14,447 --> 00:03:16,961
Molasses is about 40 percent heavier than

91
00:03:16,961 --> 00:03:17,328
water.

92
00:03:17,328 --> 00:03:18,794
So this wasn't a spill.

93
00:03:18,794 --> 00:03:19,805
It was a wave.

94
00:03:19,805 --> 00:03:22,343
Estimates vary, but many accounts put it

95
00:03:22,343 --> 00:03:25,197
around 25 feet high at first, moving at about

96
00:03:25,197 --> 00:03:26,876
35 miles an hour.

97
00:03:27,026 --> 00:03:27,970
Picture that.

98
00:03:27,970 --> 00:03:30,080
A wall of syrup taller than a two-story

99
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:32,461
building, moving faster than a car on a city

100
00:03:32,461 --> 00:03:33,440
street.

101
00:03:33,590 --> 00:03:35,030
It flattened buildings.

102
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:37,605
It ripped part of the elevated railway off its

103
00:03:37,605 --> 00:03:38,109
supports.

104
00:03:38,109 --> 00:03:40,493
It knocked a firehouse off its foundation.

105
00:03:40,493 --> 00:03:42,978
Wagons, horses and people were swept away or

106
00:03:42,978 --> 00:03:43,373
buried.

107
00:03:43,373 --> 00:03:45,823
At the crushed firehouse, rescuers spent hours

108
00:03:45,823 --> 00:03:48,167
cutting through the floorboards to reach the

109
00:03:48,167 --> 00:03:49,833
men trapped beneath.

110
00:03:49,983 --> 00:03:52,789
Inside that firehouse, men from Engine 31 had

111
00:03:52,789 --> 00:03:55,033
been eating lunch and playing cards.

112
00:03:55,033 --> 00:03:57,421
One of them, firefighter George Layhe, was

113
00:03:57,421 --> 00:04:00,579
pinned beneath the floor, and didn't survive.

114
00:04:00,729 --> 00:04:03,677
And that was just the first few seconds.

115
00:04:03,827 --> 00:04:06,477
Because the molasses was about to do something

116
00:04:06,477 --> 00:04:07,711
even worse.

117
00:04:11,261 --> 00:04:14,072
When molasses flows fast, it acts almost like

118
00:04:14,072 --> 00:04:14,635
a liquid.

119
00:04:14,635 --> 00:04:17,408
But once that wave spread out and hit the cold

120
00:04:17,408 --> 00:04:19,337
January air, it started to cool.

121
00:04:19,337 --> 00:04:21,762
And cold molasses thickens.

122
00:04:21,912 --> 00:04:24,511
People who survived the impact were now stuck

123
00:04:24,511 --> 00:04:26,245
in it, like flies on flypaper.

124
00:04:26,245 --> 00:04:28,908
The more they struggled, the more tired they

125
00:04:28,908 --> 00:04:29,151
got.

126
00:04:29,151 --> 00:04:31,811
Rescuers wading in got stuck too.

127
00:04:31,961 --> 00:04:34,122
Researchers estimate the cold made the

128
00:04:34,122 --> 00:04:36,226
molasses at least four times thicker.

129
00:04:36,226 --> 00:04:38,893
Even trapped horses couldn't be freed, and had

130
00:04:38,893 --> 00:04:40,363
to be put down.

131
00:04:40,513 --> 00:04:43,060
Sailors from a nearby training ship were among

132
00:04:43,060 --> 00:04:45,275
the first to rush in, along with police,

133
00:04:45,275 --> 00:04:46,991
firefighters and the Red Cross.

134
00:04:46,991 --> 00:04:49,595
They pulled people out of a brown, sticky mess

135
00:04:49,595 --> 00:04:51,293
that was waist-deep in places.

136
00:04:51,293 --> 00:04:53,707
Rescue work went on for days.

137
00:04:53,857 --> 00:04:56,430
21 people died, some of them children.

138
00:04:56,430 --> 00:04:58,707
Around 150 were injured.

139
00:04:58,707 --> 00:05:00,930
One victim wasn't found for nearly four

140
00:05:00,930 --> 00:05:01,929
months.

141
00:05:02,079 --> 00:05:04,850
Among the dead were two ten-year-olds, Maria

142
00:05:04,850 --> 00:05:07,465
Di Stasio and Pasquale Iantosca.

143
00:05:10,415 --> 00:05:12,224
Cleaning it up was a nightmare.

144
00:05:12,224 --> 00:05:14,371
Crews eventually used salt water from the

145
00:05:14,371 --> 00:05:16,570
harbor to break the molasses down, because

146
00:05:16,570 --> 00:05:18,740
fresh water barely touched it.

147
00:05:18,890 --> 00:05:21,363
The fire department brought in a fireboat and

148
00:05:21,363 --> 00:05:23,671
blasted the streets with powerful streams,

149
00:05:23,671 --> 00:05:25,095
block by block.

150
00:05:25,245 --> 00:05:27,485
The mess got tracked everywhere: onto

151
00:05:27,485 --> 00:05:29,908
streetcars, into homes, across the city.

152
00:05:29,908 --> 00:05:33,395
Boston Harbor reportedly ran brown for months.

153
00:05:33,545 --> 00:05:35,792
And people who lived in the North End for

154
00:05:35,792 --> 00:05:38,094
decades afterward swore that on hot summer

155
00:05:38,094 --> 00:05:40,722
days, you could still smell molasses.

156
00:05:40,872 --> 00:05:42,737
But the biggest fight was still ahead.

157
00:05:42,737 --> 00:05:43,755
Not with molasses.

158
00:05:43,755 --> 00:05:44,990
In court.

159
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:49,406
So who was responsible?

160
00:05:49,656 --> 00:05:52,220
The company had an answer ready: anarchists.

161
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:54,630
They claimed someone had bombed the tank.

162
00:05:54,630 --> 00:05:57,396
And in 1919, during a wave of real anarchist

163
00:05:57,396 --> 00:06:00,162
bombings, that wasn't an impossible story to

164
00:06:00,162 --> 00:06:01,076
sell.

165
00:06:01,226 --> 00:06:03,416
But the victims weren't buying it.

166
00:06:03,416 --> 00:06:06,201
119 plaintiffs, including families of the

167
00:06:06,201 --> 00:06:09,189
dead, banded together and sued United States

168
00:06:09,189 --> 00:06:10,479
Industrial Alcohol.

169
00:06:10,479 --> 00:06:12,380
It became one of the first major

170
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:14,816
class-action-style lawsuits against a big

171
00:06:14,816 --> 00:06:16,663
American corporation.

172
00:06:16,813 --> 00:06:18,784
The case dragged on for years.

173
00:06:18,784 --> 00:06:21,435
A court-appointed auditor heard testimony from

174
00:06:21,435 --> 00:06:23,798
thousands of witnesses and a long list of

175
00:06:23,798 --> 00:06:26,276
experts, including engineers and scientists

176
00:06:26,276 --> 00:06:29,182
arguing over metal, pressure and rivets.

177
00:06:29,332 --> 00:06:31,498
The auditor was Colonel Hugh Ogden.

178
00:06:31,498 --> 00:06:34,475
He spent nearly six years on the case, and in

179
00:06:34,475 --> 00:06:37,188
1925, his report rejected the bomb theory

180
00:06:37,188 --> 00:06:38,384
outright.

181
00:06:38,534 --> 00:06:41,133
In the end, the ruling was clear: there was no

182
00:06:41,133 --> 00:06:41,415
bomb.

183
00:06:41,415 --> 00:06:43,905
The tank was simply too weak for what it held,

184
00:06:43,905 --> 00:06:46,238
and the company had cut corners.

185
00:06:46,388 --> 00:06:49,776
The company was ordered to pay about $628,000

186
00:06:49,776 --> 00:06:52,788
in damages, which is millions in today's

187
00:06:52,788 --> 00:06:53,840
money.

188
00:06:55,990 --> 00:06:58,384
Here's why this story still matters, and why

189
00:06:58,384 --> 00:07:01,433
you probably benefit from it without knowing.

190
00:07:01,583 --> 00:07:04,229
After the flood, Massachusetts and then other

191
00:07:04,229 --> 00:07:06,640
states began requiring that engineers and

192
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:09,286
architects check their calculations, sign off

193
00:07:09,286 --> 00:07:11,873
on their plans, and have projects inspected.

194
00:07:11,873 --> 00:07:14,581
Rules we now take for granted in construction

195
00:07:14,581 --> 00:07:17,229
trace back, in part, to a tank of syrup that

196
00:07:17,229 --> 00:07:18,792
burst in Boston.

197
00:07:18,942 --> 00:07:21,501
Today the spot where the tank stood is a park

198
00:07:21,501 --> 00:07:22,468
with ball fields.

199
00:07:22,468 --> 00:07:24,744
The only sign of what happened is a small

200
00:07:24,744 --> 00:07:27,565
plaque that most people walk right past.

201
00:07:28,115 --> 00:07:30,901
21 lives lost, all because a company painted

202
00:07:30,901 --> 00:07:34,413
over its warning signs instead of fixing them.

203
00:07:34,563 --> 00:07:35,807
A wave of molasses.

204
00:07:35,807 --> 00:07:37,647
A cover-up story about bombers.

205
00:07:37,647 --> 00:07:40,086
And a lawsuit that helped make the buildings

206
00:07:40,086 --> 00:07:41,629
around you safer.

207
00:07:42,079 --> 00:07:43,358
No way.

208
00:07:44,108 --> 00:07:46,677
If you want more true stories that sound made

209
00:07:46,677 --> 00:07:47,477
up, subscribe.

210
00:07:47,477 --> 00:07:49,922
And if you liked this one, watch another one

211
00:07:49,922 --> 00:07:51,866
next, because they're just as wild.

